#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134937} :activity/id #uuid "e33ca737-353c-5d82-bb61-fdad7272cf57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049762 :topic/id #uuid "11c9069f-8d39-5302-bb61-6d4417f333f8" :topic/math-academy-id 2138} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135161 :tutorial/content "To understand the following work, it's a good idea to review Green's theorem.\n\nSuppose $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, and simple closed curve in $\\Bbb R^2,$ and $D$ is the region enclosed by $C.$ If we consider the two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\langle P, Q\\rangle$ to have zero $z$-component, i.e.,  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle P,\\: Q,\\:0\\rangle\n$$\n\nand the scalar functions $P$ and $Q$ have continuous partial derivatives in the interior of $D,$ then Green's theorem can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k} \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \n\nGreen's theorem relates the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf{F}$ over $D$ to the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ (i.e., the circulation) along the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/78/7837a682bfbf9d39ab03e42edee33306b53bf66be2fa11f91d2cdfa62ca9e9ac.png)\n\nGreen's theorem is a special case of another important theorem, which we'll discuss shortly. But first, we need to look at the relationship between oriented surfaces and their bounding curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "9eead752-f633-5bf3-a747-29ce56955373" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7690 :tutorial/title "A Review of Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "82a78598-8d2f-5d8f-8ae3-e776f9d83475" :step/math-academy-id 44631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521464}] :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"} :step/id #uuid "88e66c3b-74ca-5e04-acec-166bd6c75812" :step/math-academy-id 44632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521469}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "8a1dabb2-c1a5-5219-ad30-80f6604e07d5" :step/math-academy-id 44635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521475}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "852f8019-128f-5411-864f-ac77ce109b26" :step/math-academy-id 44682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135162 :tutorial/content "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe8c7f62-0786-58b3-874d-2aca76ef3ad7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7700 :tutorial/title "Using Stokes' Theorem to Evaluate Line Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "b2a1a0ff-e72c-5813-8d4e-f400e76cbbcb" :step/math-academy-id 44684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135163 :tutorial/content "We're now ready to state the main result of the lesson: \n\n*Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as **Stokes' theorem** and can be considered a generalization of Green's theorem. Let's spend some time building some intuition behind this result.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field in $\\mathbb R^3.$ Stokes' theorem states that the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf F$ on the surface $S$ equals the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary $C$ of $S.$ \n\n![](images/30/308aec6213f8dd42c4f853078c0305901bc19568bfd5132221bd72743f4aa58a.png)\n\nConsider the left-hand side of the above picture, and suppose we have a point $P$ that lies on $S.$ \n\nThe magnitude of the flux of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ through $S$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n.\n$$\n\nWe can think of this quantity as the amount of \"swirl\" of $\\mathbf F$ at $P$ that acts tangentially to $S$ at $P.$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the flux of the curl through $S$ at every point over the entire surface $S,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf n\\, \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhich we usually write as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us the left-hand side of Stokes' theorem.\n\nLet's now look at the right-hand side of our image. The macroscopic curl is simply the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ along the boundary of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the microscopic curls with the circulation, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nStokes' theorem can be summarized in a single picture as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34b39be1166c0556eaad00a473684916c0725c186829f6085db1a322da29612a.png)\n\nWe can immediately deduce a remarkable fact. Notice the value of the surface integral depends only on the circulation at the boundary. Therefore, within a given vector field, if two surfaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ have the same boundary $C,$ then their respective surface integrals will be equal!\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S_1} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_{S_2} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f145e410-13a4-5cdd-ad1d-ba95177d8355" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7709 :tutorial/title "Stokes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "f2180bbb-1f4c-5c73-b6d2-6adfe5adde9f" :step/math-academy-id 44722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135168 :tutorial/content "The **circulation** $\\Gamma$ of a three-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ along a closed, piecewise-smooth curve $C \\in \\mathbb R^3$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThis is the same definition as the two-dimensional case. Also, like the two-dimensional case, $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a closed curve $C,$ traversed once.\n\nRemember that circulation is always measured with respect to our chosen orientation for $C$ (positive or negative).\n\nLet's get some practice at computing circulation over three-dimensional curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "fa33c72b-b304-5717-a1ca-c81df764530f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7754 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "66ae9e5c-30ea-595a-becb-39e1f2dc5651" :step/math-academy-id 44883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135169 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an oriented surface $S$ (not closed) whose **boundary curve** is the closed curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/bc/bc0ac6b400535839d3571d61c07f3ac752bc4e85020f9b3e4cc08410b0023822.png)\n\nWhenever we select an orientation for $S,$ our chosen orientation automatically induces a positive orientation for $C.$\n\nFor example, if we select the upward-pointing unit normal vector shown below, the positive orientation is *counterclockwise* when viewed from above.\n\n![](images/73/7353d1a742e374e3704155e8c99cc26c62e8486f3606cba1144c35871ad3c432.png)\n\nAssume that you are standing on the curve $C$ with your head pointing upward (i.e., in the same direction as $\\mathbf n$). Then, if you traverse $C$ in the positive direction, the surface will always be on your left.\n\nFor example, if $z=f(x,y)$ is upward-oriented, the projection of the bounding curve onto the $xy$-plane runs counterclockwise when viewed from above. \n\n![](images/e9/e948c13c040a01ce46b6f715e313c8aabb2634e5871739169dd3f38e3229e768.png)\n\nNow, suppose we select the opposite orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81d5e3126c07f30b68bd2d46c4104212f6c7edf34baf65b077ad297f224ebe8a.png)\n\nBy choosing the *opposite* orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ the positive orientation for $C$ is *clockwise* when viewed from above!\n\nIf you're not convinced, grab a child's doll, a toothbrush, or any other object with some kind of head, and mark its left and right sides (be careful not to damage it!). Then, walk it around the edge of a table so that its left side is always next to the table surface. If you turn it upside down, you will see that the object must traverse the table's edge in the *opposite* direction for the table surface to stay on the left." :tutorial/id #uuid "c513ef57-b0ec-554e-9f32-2c13aa08b00c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7756 :tutorial/title "Induced Orientations of Boundary Curves"} :step/id #uuid "367db095-5578-5dfe-813e-342c9188b902" :step/math-academy-id 44885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134932}}] :activity/title "Stokes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134013}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521621} :activity/id #uuid "0dba044f-9105-5f4d-82c7-11978f1438fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049762 :topic/id #uuid "11c9069f-8d39-5302-bb61-6d4417f333f8" :topic/math-academy-id 2138} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1348fc00-336c-53bb-be7c-916496ef5d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520808} {:answer/id #uuid "55053be1-77fb-5c4b-806e-49e7c1fc512f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520958} {:answer/id #uuid "662c897b-ead7-5be2-af3c-4e8c4cf7d102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520996} {:answer/id #uuid "b48883a2-477f-5af5-a0a1-a15c7ffa7d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521168} {:answer/id #uuid "c81bffa8-6d28-5db1-a4e4-943e85fac37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521211} :answer-field/id #uuid "81425c09-b875-5245-8a44-a234fc04ecfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19360ab0-63c4-5cd8-9a4a-b29910944cd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162232" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-1\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{({t}^{2}-t)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "18ae8c42-183b-544f-aacc-4eaa3f1d420a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49efc351-4e26-566c-99c2-fe234a1dd84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520930} {:answer/id #uuid "83f896a5-bf32-5e5d-9cc6-f790b29f490e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521065} {:answer/id #uuid "96cf3b15-d8b8-5670-bef5-bd091199fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521109} {:answer/id #uuid "a99d6b81-4b37-544d-99ea-7af055e746a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521144} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed5de-3dc1-5a83-a649-aaf890348062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521149} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd517342-4196-5078-a01c-54f228cf9d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bdcc65b-8401-5044-8aa5-c9a7e37ffa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162890" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "241fd184-8019-52bd-8c7e-c4c658c038e8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4b66cf-ec3e-5130-bf78-dab2acbc861c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{x}\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520786} {:answer/id #uuid "3598a050-8338-5881-8c7e-00b829e53f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520878} {:answer/id #uuid "531bef77-5bda-58b0-b731-a0a09745931d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{x}\\,4+2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520953} {:answer/id #uuid "580a54b0-7193-5059-b069-221fd5506992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2+2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520963} {:answer/id #uuid "735bc584-a512-58b1-8842-88d84b62c851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521022} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce8cadb-6f17-5458-a146-66e868571308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22c4166d-7812-5cc0-89d7-bc30157982f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162363" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ $y=\\pm 2.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7448047a-c9c1-58cc-8181-6b27db0e6a80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97d45ae-ae12-5c14-924c-d56fbb9046f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} {:answer/id #uuid "685918e6-ec50-5f06-acad-83ff00e3c712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521000} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfe55e2-32b3-566f-a4fb-ac15bcefa97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521012} {:answer/id #uuid "7903ed28-5d25-5da8-85e5-0a207c57c64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521035} {:answer/id #uuid "c351ba7c-4c6f-5399-9980-cd006f2d48fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2becff-6366-549e-8db3-182572eab4ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee52e831-6d2f-5771-b93b-7f7fa07dc36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161926" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "840ab71a-6f7d-5c82-b51d-fda507e2a915" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d95993-6fce-50a6-9da0-c38e0b9621ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130961} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48ccdf-94f7-5b30-b9e9-c2b9d97a02fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130965} {:answer/id #uuid "6eea3a9c-148b-5bbc-b698-c42d3d976666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521016} {:answer/id #uuid "81f2f5fe-c2fa-5a14-a755-d561c640ccb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521062} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce79bec-631d-5e6e-9ada-b94ec4a0d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521062} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd2886-ea55-5ec0-b21b-dda63331dd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "008f670b-b77a-5939-9c4f-8602d138cc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162377" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb01237b-4d73-5b10-94f4-c0c4f3fdada5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521583}}] :activity/title "Stokes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520746}]}
